    "badge": "Best Compact Miter Saw Stand",
    "amazonUrl": "",
    "pros": [
      "Short folded length fits in car trunks and small closets",
      "Lighter than the full-size DWX723",
      "Same bracket style as DEWALT’s larger stands",
      "Extensions still support moderately long stock"
    ],
    "cons": [
      "Less extension reach than the full-size stands",
      "Lower capacity than the heavy-duty DWX723"
    ],
    "bottomLine": "The pick for small garages, condo projects and anyone who moves the stand in a car instead of a truck.",
    "description": "The DWX724 is the compact version of DEWALT’s stand line, and it exists for people who do not have room for a long folded beam. It folds into a noticeably shorter package that fits in a car trunk or a closet, and it weighs less than the DWX723, which helps if you work alone and carry the stand up stairs. It uses the same style of universal mounting brackets, so a saw already set up for DEWALT brackets moves across without fuss. The compromises are reach and capacity. The extensions do not go as far as the full-size stand, so very long stock, like 16-foot trim, needs an extra roller stand or a helper at the far end. And DEWALT rates it lower than the heavy-duty folding model. For trim projects in a house, cabinet installs and a homeowner’s occasional deck repair, the compact stand is the better fit for most garages."
  }
];

const schema = {
  "@context": "https://schema.org",
  "@graph": [
    {
      "@type": "BreadcrumbList",
      itemListElement: [
        {
          "@type": "ListItem",
          position: 1,
          name: "Home",
          item: "https://www.allmetalbrake.com/",
        },
        {
          "@type": "ListItem",
          position: 2,
          name: "Best Lists",
          item: "https://www.allmetalbrake.com/best-of/",
        },
        {
          "@type": "ListItem",
          position: 3,
          name: "Best Miter Saw Stand",
          item: "https://www.allmetalbrake.com/best-miter-saw-stand/",
        },
      ],
    },
    {
      "@type": "FAQPage",
      mainEntity: [
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "Do miter saw stands fit any brand of saw?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "Most do. The stands in this guide use universal mounting brackets that bolt to the holes in the saw base and clamp to the stand’s rail. They fit the majority of 10-inch and 12-inch miter saws from DEWALT, Bosch, Makita, Milwaukee, Ryobi and others. Always check the hole spacing against the bracket range, and use a plywood sub-base for the occasional saw that does not fit."
          }
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "Is a rolling miter saw stand worth the extra money?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "It is if you move the saw several times a day or own a heavy 12-inch slider. Keeping the saw mounted while you fold and roll saves lifting a heavy tool on and off repeatedly. If you set up once per job and work mostly with a 10-inch saw, a folding stand is lighter, cheaper and just as good for cutting."
          }
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "How much weight capacity do I need?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "Less than the listings suggest. Even a big sliding miter saw plus a long board weighs well under the 300 to 500 pounds that pro stands commonly list. Treat capacity as a sign of how robust the frame is, and pay more attention to stiffness, leg stance and bracket design when choosing a stand for a heavy saw."
          }
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "How long should the extension arms reach?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "Full-size stands support material out to roughly 16 feet in total, which covers standard trim and decking lengths. Compact stands reach less and suit shorter stock. If you regularly cut 16-foot boards, a full-size stand plus a roller stand under the far end gives the most stable setup, especially for heavy wet lumber."
          }
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "Can I use a workbench instead of a miter saw stand?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "Yes, if the bench is long and you add supports at the same height as the saw table. A bench alone only supports the saw itself, so long stock still droops. Roller stands set level with the table on each side fix that. For a permanent setup, a dedicated miter saw station with level wings on both sides is better still."
          }
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "What is a miter saw station?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "A miter saw station is a built-in bench, usually plywood, with the saw recessed so its table is level with long work surfaces on both sides. It supports stock along its full length, holds stop blocks for repeat cuts, and stores blades and tools underneath. It is the best option for a garage or shop where the saw never leaves."
          }
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "Do I need wheels on a miter saw stand for home use?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "Usually not. Most homeowners set up the stand in a garage or driveway and leave it there for the project. A folding stand is lighter and cheaper and stores more easily. Wheels become worth it if your saw is heavy, you work alone and have to move it between rooms or floors, or you have back trouble lifting."
          }
        }
      ],
    },
    {
      "@type": "Article",
      headline: title,
      author: authorSchema(Astro.url.pathname),
      datePublished: dates.published,
      dateModified: dates.modified,
      publisher: { "@type": "Organization", name: "All Metal Brake" },
    },
    {
      "@type": "ItemList",
      name: title,
      numberOfItems: PRODUCTS.length,
      itemListElement: PRODUCTS.map((product, index) => ({
        "@type": "ListItem",
        position: index + 1,
        name: product.name,
        url: new URL(`#${product.id}`, Astro.url).toString(),
      })),
    },
    ...PRODUCTS.map((product) => ({
      "@type": "Product",
      name: product.name,
      description: product.description,
      brand: {
        "@type": "Brand",
        name: product.name.split(" ")[0],
      },
    })),
  ],
};
