          <p>
            Read the table as a decision tree rather than a ranking. The first question is wheels or no wheels, which comes down to how often you move the saw. The second is how heavy your saw is, which decides whether a lighter stand will feel rigid. Price comes last, because the difference between a good stand and a cheap one is smaller than the cost of a few ruined pieces of hardwood trim.
          </p>

          <p>
            Broadly, the plain folding stands are the least expensive of the pro options, the rolling and gravity-rise stands cost more, and the Ryobi sits at the bottom of the range. If you already own a sturdy workbench and only cut short stock, you may not need a stand at all; a pair of roller stands at bench height will do. Everyone else will get more accurate cuts from one of these.
          </p>
