          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            11. Frequently Asked Questions
          </h2>

          <div class="space-y-6">
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Do miter saw stands fit any brand of saw?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Most do. The stands in this guide use universal mounting brackets that bolt to the holes in the saw base and clamp to the stand’s rail. They fit the majority of 10-inch and 12-inch miter saws from DEWALT, Bosch, Makita, Milwaukee, Ryobi and others. Always check the hole spacing against the bracket range, and use a plywood sub-base for the occasional saw that does not fit.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Is a rolling miter saw stand worth the extra money?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                It is if you move the saw several times a day or own a heavy 12-inch slider. Keeping the saw mounted while you fold and roll saves lifting a heavy tool on and off repeatedly. If you set up once per job and work mostly with a 10-inch saw, a folding stand is lighter, cheaper and just as good for cutting.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">How much weight capacity do I need?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Less than the listings suggest. Even a big sliding miter saw plus a long board weighs well under the 300 to 500 pounds that pro stands commonly list. Treat capacity as a sign of how robust the frame is, and pay more attention to stiffness, leg stance and bracket design when choosing a stand for a heavy saw.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">How long should the extension arms reach?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Full-size stands support material out to roughly 16 feet in total, which covers standard trim and decking lengths. Compact stands reach less and suit shorter stock. If you regularly cut 16-foot boards, a full-size stand plus a roller stand under the far end gives the most stable setup, especially for heavy wet lumber.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Can I use a workbench instead of a miter saw stand?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Yes, if the bench is long and you add supports at the same height as the saw table. A bench alone only supports the saw itself, so long stock still droops. Roller stands set level with the table on each side fix that. For a permanent setup, a dedicated miter saw station with level wings on both sides is better still.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">What is a miter saw station?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                A miter saw station is a built-in bench, usually plywood, with the saw recessed so its table is level with long work surfaces on both sides. It supports stock along its full length, holds stop blocks for repeat cuts, and stores blades and tools underneath. It is the best option for a garage or shop where the saw never leaves.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Do I need wheels on a miter saw stand for home use?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Usually not. Most homeowners set up the stand in a garage or driveway and leave it there for the project. A folding stand is lighter and cheaper and stores more easily. Wheels become worth it if your saw is heavy, you work alone and have to move it between rooms or floors, or you have back trouble lifting.
              </p>
            </div>

